Verified this independently and have a patch ready. Checking here before opening a PR, per CONTRIBUTING.

Mechanism: util_convert_type returns a view over its own local frag. In CuTe, is_rmem counts anything that is not gmem and not smem as rmem, so make_rmem_ptr on &frag is the identity and make_tensor wraps it in cute::ViewEngine<To_type*>. Both call sites in fused_moe_kernel_routine.cuh (lines 405 and 760) then read through that pointer after the function has returned, i.e. from a dead stack frame.

Local evidence so far (no GPU):
1. Host-side replay of the exact function body under ASAN (clang 18, detect_stack_use_after_return=1) reports stack-use-after-return, and the report points at 'frag' in the util_convert_type frame. With the function returning an owning cute::make_tensor<To_type>(layout) instead, the same 25 dtype/layout cases pass with values and logical element order unchanged.
2. Compile check with nvcc 13.0 against the real kernel-traits header: the call-site sequence (convert, make_tiled_copy_C, retile_S, partition_D, copy) instantiates with the patch, and engine-type static asserts read ViewEngine<ElementOutput*> before and ArrayEngine<ElementOutput, numel> after. I also checked that every per-thread accumulator layout from partition_fragment_C over the SM80 fused-MoE tile set (f16/bf16, TileM 16-128, TileN 32-256) has cosize == size, which is the contiguity the existing HACK comment already assumes.

Proposed change, replacing the make_rmem_ptr return:

    auto converted = cute::make_tensor<To_type>(tensor.layout());
    CUTLASS_PRAGMA_UNROLL
    for (int element_idx = 0; element_idx < numel; ++element_idx)
    {
        converted.data()[element_idx] = frag[element_idx];
    }
    return converted;

The element copy preserves the existing order and the caller sees the same layout; the per-thread fragment size is unchanged. I can also add static_assert(cute::cosize_v<Layout> == numel) next to the HACK comment if you want that precondition enforced at compile time.

I have not built TensorRT-LLM or run the kernel end to end, so I am not claiming the SM121 clang failure is reproduced here, only the lifetime defect and this fix.

